2026-06-05 02:48:29 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\nYou are given a list of RawRecord objects. Each RawRecord contains:\n- raw_id\n- text\n- metadata (including a \'created\' field representing when the record was published)\n\nYour task is to infer the single canonical semantic triple that best represents\nthe shared meaning across ALL records in the list.\n\nA triple has the form:\n(subject, predicate, object)\n\nRules:\n1. The triple must express the core relation that all records instantiate.\n2. The triple must be abstract enough to cover all records, but specific enough\n   to distinguish this subgraph from unrelated content.\n3. Use canonicalized forms of entities and relations.\n4. The predicate should be a relation phrase, not a full sentence.\n5. Ignore surface details and stylistic differences.\n6. Do NOT summarize the documents. Extract the underlying relation.\n7. If multiple triples are possible, choose the one that is maximally general\n   while still being true for all records.\n\nYou must also infer contextual metadata with the following keys:\n- location\n- subject_matter\n- industry\n- time  ← IMPORTANT: this should reflect WHEN THE EVENT OCCURRED,\n         not when the records were published.\n\nYou may use the \'created\' timestamps to help disambiguate event time,\nbut you must NOT output the \'created\' timestamps themselves.\n\nOnly include a metadata field if it is stable across ALL records.\nIf a field is not stable, set it to null.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4155737\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 71480797\nTEXT: The Trump administration is proposing tariffs of 10% or more on products from dozens of major trading partners, citing allegations of forced labor in the importation of goods. The USTR report found that countries such as Canada, Mexico, Taiwan, and the UK would face 10% additional tariffs for allegedly failing to enforce a forced labor import ban. The report also noted that some key items, including textiles, tomatoes, bananas, coffee, and metals, would be exempt or subject to lower tariffs. The administration plans to appeal a federal judge\'s order making companies eligible for refunds on earlier tariffs, and has proposed 25% tariffs on imports from Brazil, citing "unreasonable" trade practices.\n\nID: 4155831\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: us-proposes-new-tariffs-over-failure-to-act-on-forced-labor-222835\nTEXT: The US proposes new tariffs on Canada, Mexico, Taiwan, the UK, and other countries for allegedly failing to enforce a forced labor import ban. A 10% tariff will be imposed on Canada, Mexico, Taiwan, and the UK, while a 12.5% tariff will be imposed on China, Japan, India, South Korea, Brazil, and Switzerland. USTR Ambassador Jamieson Greer stated that the failure to address forced labor imports creates an "unlevel playing field" for American workers. This move follows a recent tariff deal between the US and EU, which capped tariffs on most EU exports at 15%.\n\nID: 4155671\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: us-plans-extra-tariffs-for-60-countries-over-forced-labor\nTEXT: The US Trade Representative (USTR) has initiated 60 investigations into forced labor, leading to proposed tariffs on imports from 60 countries. The alleged failure to prohibit imports made with forced labor is seen as unfair competition, subjecting US producers to unfair market conditions. The USTR will hold hearings on July 7 regarding the proposed tariffs, which may unsettle several US trade partners who have been negotiating lower levies for over a year. The EU has recently approved a trade deal with Washington, capping tariffs on most EU exports at 15%.\n\nID: 4155791\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: trump-new-levies\nTEXT: The US is proposing new tariffs of at least 10% on imports from 60 trading partners, including Canada, Mexico, the European Union, Taiwan and the UK. The 10% rate applies to goods from economies that impose prohibitions on forced labor imports or have committed to doing so. In contrast, products from economies that have failed to impose and effectively enforce such prohibitions would be subject to a 12.5% levy, including China, India, Japan, South Korea, Brazil and Switzerland.\n\nID: 4155707\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 2607717\nTEXT: The US Trade Representative (USTR) has proposed new tariffs on Korea and 53 other economies for failing to prevent imports made with forced labor. An investigative report found that 54 economies, including Korea, China, Japan, Britain, Australia and Taiwan, lack sufficient laws, institutions and enforcement mechanisms to prevent the importation of goods produced with forced labor. This move aims to address human rights abuses in supply chains, particularly in the textile and garment industries.\n\nID: 4156131\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 667081-us-proposes-tariffs-on-60-economies-including-pakistan-over-forced-labour-concerns\nTEXT: The US Trade Representative has proposed new tariffs targeting 60 economies, including Pakistan and India, for alleged failures to act against forced labour. The proposed tariffs range from 10% to 12.5%, with exemptions for goods such as beef, coffee, and certain fruits and nuts. Canada and Mexico will also be exempt due to their compliance with the North American free trade pact. The public is invited to provide written comments by July 6, and the USTR will hold hearings.\n\nID: 4156288\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: european-commission-slams-donald-trump-forced-labor-tariffs-as-unjustified\nTEXT: The European Commission has slammed US Trade Representative\'s Office for imposing tariffs on goods made with forced labor, citing the move as unjustified. The US Trade Representative\'s Office had proposed re-imposing a 10% tariff on key trading partners, including the European Union, Canada and Mexico. However, the European Commission argues that this move is not justified as it fails to address the root cause of forced labor in these countries.\n\nID: 4155749\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 507-62a7ce17-8a2e-42b3-ae4c-1cfc68256145\nTEXT: The US Trade Representative has proposed 10% additional tariffs on imports from Canada, Mexico, Taiwan, and the UK due to allegations of forced labor enforcement failures. The report cites specific countries as failing to prevent modern slavery and human trafficking in their supply chains, resulting in increased tariffs on goods from these nations.\n\nID: 4155848\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 1404650-trump-threatens-tariffs-on-60-countries-as-forced-labour-concerns-intensify\nTEXT: Donald Trump has threatened tariffs of 10-12.5% on 60 countries, including the UK, EU and Australia, amid concerns over forced labor. The administration is using Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974 to penalize countries over alleged failures to stop the import of goods produced by forced labor.\n\n\n',)
