2026-06-05 03:05:33 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\nYou are given a list of RawRecord objects. Each RawRecord contains:\n- raw_id\n- text\n- metadata (including a \'created\' field representing when the record was published)\n\nYour task is to infer the single canonical semantic triple that best represents\nthe shared meaning across ALL records in the list.\n\nA triple has the form:\n(subject, predicate, object)\n\nPrioritize:\n- COMMONALITY: identify the entities, actions, and objects that appear consistently\n  across the majority of records.\n- DETAIL: include specific details that are stable across many records, not just vague\n  abstractions.\n- REPRESENTATIVENESS: ignore outlier phrasing or rare details that appear in only a few\n  records.\n- CANONICAL FORM: normalize phrasing so the triple is concise and domain‑appropriate.\n\nAvoid:\n- overly generic triples (e.g., "Organization engages in activity")\n- summarizing the documents\n- including details that are not shared across most records\n\nYou must also infer contextual metadata with the following keys:\n- location\n- subject_matter\n- industry\n- time  ← this reflects WHEN THE EVENT OCCURRED, not when the records were published.\n\nYou may use the \'created\' timestamps to help disambiguate event time,\nbut you must NOT output the \'created\' timestamps themselves.\n\nOnly include a metadata field if it is stable across ALL records.\nIf a field is not stable, set it to null.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4156381\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: microsoft-scout-ushers-in-new-category-of-ai-autopilots\nTEXT: Microsoft Scout is the first entry in a new category of AI autopilots, according to corporate vice president Omar Shahine. Autopilots are agents that work autonomously, have their own identity and act on the user\'s behalf. They can proactively schedule meeting times across time zones, flag important meetings and generate materials needed for preparation. Autopilots can also identify upcoming deliverables, block time on the calendar and spot risks such as stalled decisions. They operate in the background, understand how work gets done across apps and systems, and take action without needing to be prompted. This technology is similar to personal AI agents being built by Google and Meta, which operate in the background, handle tasks without being asked twice and get sharper at anticipating needs over time.\n\nID: 4156215\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: deepseek-nears-deal-to-raise-7-4-billion-for-open-source-ai\nTEXT: DeepSeek is nearing a deal to raise $7.4 billion for its open-source AI models, with founder Liang Wenfeng committing to continue developing these models while working towards achieving artificial general intelligence (AGI). This move comes as AI companies, including those in China, face increasing pressure to show results following hundreds of billions of dollars in infrastructure investments. DeepSeek\'s "research-first philosophy" is being explored by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic, which are also looking into stock market listings and multiple revenue paths. The company\'s innovative approach to training large language models, which shows improved performance without a proportional increase in training costs, could alter the economics of AI deployment in sectors that rely on cost discipline.\n\nID: 4155950\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: microsofts-big-new-idea-for-ai-gadgets-is-a-badge-with-a-camera-2000766506\nTEXT: Microsoft\'s new AI gadget concept, the "smart access badge," is a wearable device with a camera that can take pictures, clean them up, and share them with teams. The badge is geared towards enterprise applications in healthcare and retail, allowing workers to use the integrated camera to better understand their environment. The device can also pair with a machine via Bluetooth, enabling users to "hand off tasks between the devices and keep lock state consistent." Microsoft envisions a separate concept device, a "companion" for the PC that sits on a desk and features a touchscreen, microphone, and presence sensors. This device can help with tasks such as order fulfillment in Amazon\'s factories.\n\nID: 4156069\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 2209\nTEXT: Microsoft has unveiled Project Solara, a line of AI devices that don\'t require traditional operating systems or apps. The Surface RTX Spark Dev Box, one of the devices, uses an Nvidia chip to bring AI directly to PCs. It was demonstrated running a 120 billion-parameter AI model, which is too complex for most PCs to load. The new machines are priced to compete with Apple\'s premium offerings and have helped boost shares of Microsoft and PC makers like Dell Technologies. To make OpenClaw, a popular open-source software in China, safe for businesses to use on computers with sensitive corporate data, Microsoft is developing new tools.\n\nID: 4155949\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: microsoft-is-exploiting-legal-fears-to-sell-its-powerful-new-ai-model-to-businesses-2000766632\nTEXT: Microsoft is exploiting legal fears to sell its powerful new AI model, MAI-Thinking-1, to businesses by emphasizing the importance of tracking data lineage and avoiding distillation. The model, which was trained "entirely from the bottom" without distillation, scored 97% on the AIME benchmark and 53% on SWE Bench Pro. This approach is a departure from its previous partnership with OpenAI, and Microsoft is positioning itself as a leader in AI by prioritizing human interests and agency. The company\'s pitch to enterprise customers is that distillation can lead to trouble down the road due to uncertainty and legal controversy around sourcing training data.\n\nID: 4156540\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: article-87b71e46-4caa-4221-ba66-6cca7b070fac-html\nTEXT: Snowflake and Amazon Web Services (AWS) have agreed on a 6 billion dollar deal. The latest agreement builds on their momentum with deeper product integrations across generative and agentic AI, expanded go-to-market through AWS Marketplace, and joint investments in customer-success programs, workload migrations, and strategic industry solutions. CEO Sridhar Ramaswamy stated that AI has generated enormous excitement, but for enterprises, the real challenge and opportunity is turning intelligence into action. The partnership aims to help organizations reason over trusted data, coordinate workflows, and drive real business outcomes.\n\nID: 4156140\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: big-tech-is-terrified-of-ai-agents-wiping-out-ad-revenue-says-billions-network-ceo\nTEXT: Big Tech Fears AI Agents Wiping Out Ad Revenue, Says Billions Network CEO. \n\nBillions Network\'s CEO is concerned that AI agents could significantly reduce ad revenue, potentially impacting the entire tech industry. The expert\'s cautious stance is reflected in the recent Bitcoin price stability at $67,000, which faces a critical juncture after sliding 9.5% in seven days. The UK\'s financial watchdog has also cracked down on Premier League crypto partnerships, highlighting the growing regulatory scrutiny in the industry.\n\nID: 4156343\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: for-lawyers-ai-may-increase-work-negotiating-contracts\nTEXT: A growing number of law firms are adopting AI-native practices, which may lead to increased work for lawyers in contract negotiations. This shift is driven by the need for operational control and the use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in various industries. As a result, CEOs are facing career risks and regulatory roadblocks due to the lack of governance structure in AI adoption. A proposed class action lawsuit targets Meta and Mark Zuckerberg, with the added concern of research scientists allegedly carrying out mass piracy of books.\n\nID: 4156488\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 1\nTEXT: India\'s data-rich market fuels AI training, but lacks homegrown innovation leveraging this advantage. The country\'s vast amount of data creates a fertile ground for AI training, with many startups and companies investing heavily in this area. However, despite having a large pool of data, India is struggling to produce innovative AI solutions that can compete with global giants. This lack of homegrown innovation is hindering the country\'s ability to fully leverage its data-rich market and remain competitive in the AI space.\n\nID: 4156200\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: a-new-approach-microsoft-ceo-claims-its-ai-superfactory-will-use-the-same-amount-of-water-each-year-as-a-neighborhood-restaurant\nTEXT: Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella claims its AI Superfactory, Fairwater, will use the same amount of water each year as a neighborhood restaurant. The facility, opened in September 2025, features improved cooling efficiency with its cooling loop filled once and operating with effectively zero ongoing water consumption. This design allows for dense packing of GPUs while preserving fast network access, making it a massive singular AI machine.\n\nID: 4155966\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: two-years-after-copilot-nvidia-is-shoving-ai-into-pcs-again-2000766278\nTEXT: Nvidia\'s RTX Spark systems, designed to be agent-ready ARM PCs, may have a competitive edge in gaming due to their GPU capabilities. This is contrary to Jensen Huang\'s claims that Snapdragon-based systems, such as those with 50 available "claws" (processing units), can also run AI agents effectively. RTX Spark systems may offer more control over processing power for AI agents, a feature that Qualcomm has struggled to achieve.\n\n\n',)
