2026-06-05 03:16:51 [DEBUG] llm.engine: ARGS: ('\nYou are given a list of RawRecord objects. Each RawRecord contains:\n- raw_id\n- text\n- metadata (including a \'created\' field representing when the record was published)\n\nYour task is to infer the single canonical semantic triple that best represents\nthe shared meaning across ALL records in the list.\n\nA triple has the form:\n(subject, predicate, object)\n\nPrioritize:\n- COMMONALITY: identify the entities, actions, and objects that appear consistently\n  across the majority of records.\n- DETAIL: include specific details that are stable across many records, not just vague\n  abstractions.\n- REPRESENTATIVENESS: ignore outlier phrasing or rare details that appear in only a few\n  records.\n- CANONICAL FORM: normalize phrasing so the triple is concise and domain‑appropriate.\n\nAvoid:\n- overly generic triples (e.g., "Organization engages in activity")\n- summarizing the documents\n- including details that are not shared across most records\n\nYou must also infer contextual metadata with the following keys:\n- location\n- subject_matter\n- industry\n- time  ← this reflects WHEN THE EVENT OCCURRED, not when the records were published.\n\nYou may use the \'created\' timestamps to help disambiguate event time,\nbut you must NOT output the \'created\' timestamps themselves.\n\nOnly include a metadata field if it is stable across ALL records.\nIf a field is not stable, set it to null.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4156088\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: 2190\nTEXT: El Niño conditions are set to influence global temperature and rainfall patterns, increasing the risk of extreme weather. According to UN Secretary-General António Guterres, the sea-surface temperature in the central-eastern Equatorial Pacific was approaching El Niño thresholds from late April to mid-May. This is due to unusually warm subsurface conditions across the tropical Pacific, with temperatures exceeding 6 °C above average. The Southern Oscillation Index is also consistent with developing El Niño conditions, indicating a substantial reservoir of heat contributing to the observed surface warming.\n\nID: 4155586\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: wmo-el-nino-could-trigger-drought-in-indonesia-in-coming-months\nTEXT: The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) warns that El Niño could trigger drought in Indonesia in the coming months. WMO Secretary-General Celeste Saulo emphasizes the need for countries to prepare for a potentially strong El Niño event, which will exacerbate drought and heavy rainfall, increasing the risk of heatwaves on land and in the ocean. The 2023-2024 El Niño event was among the five strongest on record, contributing to record global temperatures in 2024. Preparedness and early warning systems are crucial to protect communities and reduce economic losses, according to Saulo.\n\nID: 4156194\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: un-warns-world-to-prepare-for-el-nino-extreme-weather\nTEXT: The United Nations\' World Meteorological Organization (WMO) warns the world to prepare for an extreme El Nino weather event, with 80% probability of developing in June-August. This natural climate phenomenon warms surface temperatures in the central and eastern equatorial Pacific Ocean, leading to changes in winds, pressure, and rainfall patterns. The WMO forecasts a pronounced shift toward El Nino conditions, with 90% likelihood of developing by November. This could exacerbate drought and heavy rainfall, increasing the risk of heatwaves on land and in the ocean.\n\nID: 4155581\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: el-nino-could-push-temperatures-higher-un-warns\nTEXT: El Niño could push temperatures higher, warns UN. The World Meteorological Organization (WMO) notes that sea-surface temperature in the central-eastern Equatorial Pacific was approaching El Niño thresholds in late April to mid-May, with sub-surface temperatures over 6 degrees Celsius above average. This coincides with typically high temperatures in parts of Asia, such as India and China. For June to August, forecasts project "a nearly universal dominance of above normal temperatures in nearly all parts of the globe."\n\n\n',)
