ourt Philippe Chatrier. Chwalinska, 24, became only the second qualifier in the Open era to reach the Roland Garros semi-finals and the sixth to achieve the feat at a Grand Slam. She will face world number one Aryna Sabalenka or Russia\'s Diana Shnaider for a place in Saturday\'s final. Chwalinska won only two Tour-level matches on clay before this tournament, but rediscovered her composure at the perfect moment to seal victory.\n\nID: 4155796\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: french-open-russia-ukraine\nTEXT: The French Open semifinals have taken on a complex geopolitical tone, with emotionally charged matches unfolding on the clay courts of Roland Garros. Marta Kostyuk defeated Elina Svitolina 6-3, 2-6, 6-2 on Court Philippe-Chatrier, advancing to a semi-final against Mirra Andreeva. Andreeva had previously defeated Sorana Cirstea 6-0, 6-3. The draw\'s other side also carries political overtones, with the Russian invasion of Ukraine now in its fifth year.\n\nID: 4155622\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: french-open-2026-sabalenka-defeats-osaka-blockbuster-night-match-reach-quarter-finals-87296\nTEXT: Artemiya Sabalenka defeated Naomi Osaka in a blockbuster night match at the French Open, advancing to the quarter finals. This is Sabalenka\'s first time reaching the fourth round in Paris, a notable milestone in her Roland Garros career. Osaka\'s performance was her strongest yet at the clay-court major, but she departed without advancing further.\n\nID: 4155616\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: mirra-andreeva-sweeps-past-cirstea-reach-french-open-semi-finals-again-87305\nTEXT: Mirra Andreeva has reached the French Open semi-finals for the second time, defeating Sorana Cirstea 6-0, 6-3 in just 56 minutes on Court Philippe-Chatrier. Andreeva\'s dominant display, which included a quick 24-minute opening set, sent her into the semi-finals and further solidified her position as a leading contender in the women\'s draw.\n\n\n',)
