hat over 90% of homeowners will see a significant cut. The plan, which is close to approval, would raise the homestead exemption to $150,000 in 2027 and then up to $250,000 in 2028. Critics argue that the plan is a tax shift rather than a cut, and that someone will end up paying. Florida State Reps. Robin Bartleman and Tom Fabricio support the plan, which would save thousands of dollars for average homeowners but result in hundreds of millions of dollars lost for cities and counties.\n\nID: 4156048\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nSLUG: florida-lawmakers-pass-desantis-property-tax-bill-sending-it-to-voters-for-their-approval\nTEXT: Florida lawmakers have passed a property tax bill, sending it to voters for approval. State Sen. Barbara Sharief (D) Miramar expressed concerns that the bill could "destroy our local governments and county governments." Pembroke Pines Mayor Angelo Castillo warned that the bill\'s provision of property tax relief could compromise local services such as public safety, transportation, and mosquito spraying. Officials fear that these essential services could be at risk if the bill is approved.\n\n\n',)
