lable jobs in the US rose sharply in April, reaching an estimated 7.62 million positions, the highest level since nearly two years ago. However, job openings may not necessarily turn into job offers due to the low-hire, low-fire dynamic in the labor market. The imbalance between employers\' wish lists and their payrolls can be attributed to rising labor costs and economic uncertainty. Despite this, some experts see the surge in white-collar job openings as a welcome sign for job seekers, particularly those in industries that have been contracting. The professional and business services industry saw over 90% of the increase in April job openings, which could indicate that the US labor market is stabilizing and possibly expanding.\n\nID: 4155606\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: tempo.co\nSLUG: us-job-openings-hit-two-year-high-despite-weak-hiring\nTEXT: US job openings hit a two-year high in April, exceeding the number of unemployed workers for the first time since June 2024. The professional and business services sector accounted for over 90% of the increase in vacancies, driven by continued demand for skilled workers. Despite concerns about artificial intelligence reducing employment opportunities, job seekers such as college graduates and white-collar workers may find encouragement in the April increase. However, employers remain cautious about making hiring decisions due to rising labor costs and uncertain economic conditions, with some warning that miscalculating on the wrong worker can be costly.\n\n\n',)
