s to regulate Name, Image, and Likeness (NIL) payments, but experts claim it will only shift the issue to the federal level. According to Ross Dellenger\'s analysis, schools have $21.3 million to spend on athletes per year, but many redirect corporate sponsor cash to their rosters as "third-party NIL" compensation. This move is seen as a way for programs to gain an advantage in recruiting, but critics believe the cap will only push these payments into the shadows.\n\nID: 4156257\nCREATED: 2026-06-03\nDOMAIN: scrippsnews.com\nSLUG: lawmakers-push-federal-nil-standards-as-college-sports-face-growing-challenges\nTEXT: Lawmakers push federal NIL standards as college sports face growing challenges. The proposed bill aims to address issues beyond Name, Image, and Likeness (NIL) by changing media broadcast rules, ensuring athletes have medical coverage, and protecting women\'s and Olympic sports. Cunningham emphasizes the importance of funding for the Olympic movement, stating that 75% of US Olympians are college athletes and highlighting the need for government support.\n\n\n',)
